<header><p class="st-kicker">tribal confederation · Balkans and Hungary</p><h1 class="st-title">Costoboci</h1><p class="st-meta">50 CE – 200 CE (approx.)</p></header>
<p class="st-teaser">Dacian people north of the Carpathians whose daring raid of 170 CE reached Greece and sacked the sanctuary of Eleusis.</p>
<div class="st-body"><div class="st-text"><h2>Origins</h2><p>Known mainly from Roman sources, the Costoboci were a Dacian people living beyond Roman <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dacia">Dacia</a>, probably in the upper <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dniester">Dniester</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/prut">Prut</a> region of modern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ukraine">Ukraine</a> and <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/kingdom-of-romania">Romania</a>, in the 1st and 2nd centuries CE. An inscription in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rome">Rome</a> names a Costobocian king, Pieporus.</p><h2>Rule and society</h2><p>Little is known of their society beyond Roman notices. During the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/marcomannic-wars">Marcomannic Wars</a>, in about 170 CE, they broke through the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/danube">Danube</a> frontier, swept through <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/moesia">Moesia</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/thrace">Thrace</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/macedonia-region">Macedonia</a>, and plundered the mysteries sanctuary at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/eleusis">Eleusis</a> near <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/empire/athens">Athens</a>.</p><h2>Decline and legacy</h2><p>Soon after the raid they were attacked by the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hasdingi">Asding</a> <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vandals">Vandals</a>, who took their lands. They vanish from the record by the end of the 2nd century, probably absorbed by the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/carpi">Carpi</a> and other neighbours.</p><p class="st-conf">Few sources survive for this state; details are uncertain.</p></div><aside class="st-side"><figure class="st-fig"><img src="/images/states/costoboci.jpg" alt="Costoboci" width="320" height="277"><figcaption>Image: ArdadN, uploaded at Commons by El_Bes · CC BY-SA 3.0 ro · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Roman_province_of_Dacia_(106_-_271_AD).svg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">source</a></figcaption></figure><dl class="st-facts"><dt>Years</dt><dd>50 CE – 200 CE (approx.)</dd><dt>Centuries</dt><dd><a href="/centuries/1st-century">1st century</a> – <a href="/centuries/3rd-century">3rd century</a></dd><dt>Type</dt><dd>tribal confederation</dd><dt>Character</dt><dd>tribal</dd><dt>Rulers</dt><dd>Pieporus</dd><dt>Religion</dt><dd>Dacian polytheism</dd><dt>Language</dt><dd>Dacian</dd><dt>Today</dt><dd><a href="/countries/romania">Romania</a>, <a href="/countries/ukraine">Ukraine</a></dd><dt>Also</dt><dd>Costobocians</dd></dl><figure class="st-locator"><a href="/atlas?year=125&amp;focus=costoboci" aria-label="Costoboci on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="5272 558 640 512" role="img" aria-label="Map: where Costoboci lay"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M5592 814h0"/><path class="map-dot-ap" d="M5592 814h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>Location approximate: shown in present-day Romania. <a href="/atlas?year=125&amp;focus=costoboci">On the atlas &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></aside></div>

